architecture:
architecture: Dual-Branch 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) with one-hot encoded RNA sequences and optional
categorical embeddings for genomic metadata
hyperparameters: {
  "model": "Dual-Branch 1D CNN",
  "gene_branch": {
    "input_dim": 4,
    "seq_len": 50,
    "conv1d": [ {
      "filters": 64,
      "kernel_size": 3,
      "activation": "relu",
      "padding": "valid",
      "l2_reg": 1e-5
    } ],
    "pooling": "global_max"
  },
  "noncoding_branch": {
    "input_dim": 4,
    "seq_len": 26,
    "conv1d": [ {
      "filters": 64,
      "kernel_size": 3,
      "activation": "relu",
      "padding": "valid",
      "l2_reg": 1e-5
    } ],
    "pooling": "global_max"
  },
  "merged": {
    "dense_layers": [ {
      "units": 64,
      "activation": "relu",
      "l2_reg": 1e-4,
      "dropout": 0.5
    } ],
    "output": {
      "units": 1,
      "activation": "sigmoid"
    }
  },
  "optimizer": {
    "type": "Adam",
    "learning_rate": 1e-3,
    "beta_1": 0.9,
    "beta_2": 0.999,
    "epsilon": 1e-07,
    "weight_decay": 1e-5
  },
  "loss": "binary_crossentropy",
  "metrics": ["accuracy", "AUC", "Recall", "Precision"],
  "training": {
    "epochs": 30,
    "batch_size": 128,
    "validation_split": 0.2,
    "shuffle": true,
    "early_stopping": {
      "monitor": "val_loss",
      "patience": 5,
      "restore_best_weights": true
    }
  }
}
reasoning: The primary predictive signal is the nucleotide complementarity between the constant-length gene
fragment and the variable-length noncoding RNA. For each sequence we apply a 1‑D CNN branch
consisting of two 1‑D convolution layers followed by global_max pooling to produce a compact
representation of local motifs. The two embeddings from gene (length 50) and noncoding (length 26)
branches are concatenated and passed through a dense layer with dropout for regularisation. Adam
optimisation with a learning rate of 1e‑3 and early stopping on val_loss will effectively train the
small dataset (~8 k samples).
